---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'gap', label: 'The service everyone skips testing' },
  { id: 'start', label: 'Start BigQuery on localhost' },
  { id: 'query', label: 'Create, load and query' },
  { id: 'coverage', label: 'What you can cover locally' },
];
const startBq = `localcloud start --local-only --services bigquery
eval "$(localcloud env)"`;
const pyBq = `import os
from google.auth.credentials import AnonymousCredentials
from google.cloud import bigquery

client = bigquery.Client(
    project=os.environ["GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT"],
    credentials=AnonymousCredentials(),
    client_options={"api_endpoint": os.environ["BIGQUERY_EMULATOR_HOST"]},
)

client.query("CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS analytics_local").result()
client.query(
    "CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE analytics_local.orders (customer STRING, total NUMERIC)"
).result()
errors = client.insert_rows_json(
    f"{client.project}.analytics_local.orders",
    [
        {"customer": "ada", "total": "120.50"},
        {"customer": "grace", "total": "310.00"},
        {"customer": "ada", "total": "45.25"},
    ],
)
assert not errors, errors

sql = """
SELECT customer,
       SUM(total) AS spend,
       RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SUM(total) DESC) AS position
FROM analytics_local.orders
GROUP BY customer
ORDER BY position
"""
for row in client.query(sql).result():
    print(row.position, row.customer, row.spend)`;
const output = `1 grace 310
2 ada 165.75`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="missing-bigquery-emulator-local-sql"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="Google publishes local emulators for several of its databases and for Pub/Sub. BigQuery isn't one of them, so the SQL that feeds your dashboards is often the least tested code you ship. LocalCloud includes a BigQuery engine that the standard client talks to."
  primary={{ href: '/services/bigquery/', label: 'BigQuery service guide' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/docs/bigquery-emulator-features/', label: 'BigQuery feature reference' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="A table created with DDL, rows loaded through the streaming API, and a window-function query answered on localhost by the standard Python client."
  needs="Python with google-cloud-bigquery"
  next={{ posts: ['event-driven-pipelines-gcs-pubsub-bigquery', 'run-dataproc-locally-docker'], doc: { href: '/docs/bigquery-emulator-features/', label: 'BigQuery feature reference: functions, DDL and the Storage API' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="gap">The service everyone skips testing</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">You can run Pub/Sub or Spanner on a laptop with a tool from Google. For BigQuery the choice has been a real project, with credentials and a bill for every query, or no test at all.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Most teams pick no test. Transforms get checked by eye in the console, schema migrations are run once and trusted, and the first sign of a wrong cast or a renamed column is a broken report.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="start">Start BigQuery on localhost</h2>
    <BlogCode label="Start BigQuery" code={startBq} copy="copy-blog-bq-start" />
    <p class="mt-4">The engine runs inside the LocalCloud container. It parses GoogleSQL and executes it on DuckDB, a columnar engine that suits analytical queries. <code>localcloud env</code> exports <code>BIGQUERY_EMULATOR_HOST</code>; the Python client takes that value through <code>client_options</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="query">Create, load and query</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">This script does what a real pipeline test does: build a table, load rows through <code>insertAll</code>, and assert on the result of a query that uses an aggregate and a window function.</p>
    <BlogCode label="test_bigquery_local.py" code={pyBq} copy="copy-blog-bq-python" />
    <BlogCode label="Output" code={output} />
    <p class="mt-4">No project, no credentials, no query charge. Swap the SQL for one of your own transforms and you have its first integration test.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="coverage">What you can cover locally</h2>
    <ul class="site-list mt-5">
      <li><strong>Schemas and migrations.</strong> Datasets, tables and views, partitioned and clustered definitions, and <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA</code> queries to check them.</li>
      <li><strong>Query logic.</strong> CTEs, <code>UNNEST</code>, window functions, JSON functions and scripting.</li>
      <li><strong>Loading.</strong> Streaming inserts for rows as they arrive, and load jobs for batches.</li>
      <li><strong>Exploration.</strong> A SQL editor in the <a href="/docs/console/#sql-and-service-explorers">web console</a> for trying a query before it goes into code.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">The <a href="/docs/bigquery-emulator-features/">feature reference</a> lists function coverage in detail. Query cost and performance at warehouse scale are things only BigQuery itself can tell you, so measure those there.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
